---
title: 'How to Reject Candidates Kindly: Rejection Email Templates and Candidate Experience Tips'
publishedAt: 2026-11-11
type: companies
---
**Quick answer:** Reject candidates promptly, clearly and kindly. Send a rejection within a week of deciding, use the candidate's name and the role, say plainly that you won't be moving forward, thank them for their time, and, for candidates who interviewed, offer one or two specific, job-related points of feedback if your policy allows. Personalise rejections after interviews; templates are fine for early-stage applicants. Never ghost candidates: silence damages your employer brand more than a "no" does.

Every role you fill means rejecting many more people than you hire. How you do it shapes what candidates say about you, whether they apply again, whether they refer friends, and, for consumer companies, whether they stay customers. Being "ghosted" after applying or interviewing is one of the most common complaints job seekers have.

## Why rejection emails matter

- **Your reputation spreads.** Engineers talk, and they post on review sites and in communities.
- **Today's rejected candidate is tomorrow's hire.** Someone who wasn't right for this role may be perfect for the next one.
- **Silence costs more than a "no".** Candidates keep following up, and your team spends time on replies anyway.
- **It's the respectful thing to do.** People put real effort into applications and interviews.

## When should you send a rejection?

{% table %}
- Stage
- When to reject
- How
---
- After applying (not shortlisted)
- Within 1–2 weeks of the decision
- Template email
---
- After a screening call or AI screening interview
- Within a week
- Template with light personalisation
---
- After technical or onsite interviews
- Within 2–3 working days
- Personal email or a call
---
- After a final round
- Within 1–2 working days
- A phone call, then an email
{% /table %}

The later the stage, the more personal the rejection should be.

## What a good rejection email includes

1. **The candidate's name and the role.**
2. **A clear decision** in the first two sentences. Don't bury it.
3. **Thanks** for their time and effort.
4. **Feedback**, for candidates who interviewed, if you can share it.
5. **What happens next**: whether you'll keep their details for future roles.

What to leave out:

- Vague, misleading hope ("we may be in touch soon") if you won't be.
- Comparisons with other candidates.
- Anything about age, family, health, nationality or other protected characteristics.
- Long explanations of your internal process.

## Template 1: After applying (not shortlisted)

**Subject:** Your application for Frontend Engineer at Fleetline

> Hi Ananya,
>
> Thank you for applying for the Frontend Engineer role at Fleetline. We've reviewed all applications carefully, and we won't be moving forward with yours for this role.
>
> We received many strong applications, and this decision isn't a reflection of your overall abilities. We'd be glad to see you apply for future roles that match your experience.
>
> Thanks again for your interest in Fleetline.
>
> Rahul, Engineering

## Template 2: After a screening interview

**Subject:** Update on your Backend Engineer application

> Hi Marco,
>
> Thank you for taking the time to speak with us about the Backend Engineer role. After reviewing your interview, we've decided not to move forward at this stage.
>
> For this role we're specifically looking for more experience running services in production at high traffic. Your API design work was clear and well thought through.
>
> We'll keep your details on file and reach out if a role that's a better fit opens up.
>
> Best,
> Priya

## Template 3: After a final interview

Call first if you can, then follow with:

**Subject:** Thank you, and our decision on the Senior Engineer role

> Hi Sam,
>
> Thanks again for the time you spent with the team over the last few weeks. As I mentioned on the phone, we've decided to offer the role to another candidate whose experience with event streaming was a closer match for what we need right now.
>
> The team was impressed by your system design session, especially how you handled the caching trade-offs. If you're open to it, I'd like to stay in touch: we expect to hire for our platform team early next year.
>
> Thank you for your interest in us,
> Lena

## Template 4: When the role is cancelled or put on hold

> Hi Jo,
>
> I wanted to let you know that we've paused hiring for the Data Engineer role due to a change in plans. This isn't about your application, which we liked.
>
> If the role reopens, I'll contact you first. Thank you for your patience and interest.

## Template 5: After a knockout question

> Hi Ravi,
>
> Thank you for applying for the Product Engineer role. This role requires working from our Bengaluru office twice a week, and your application indicated that isn't possible, so we can't move forward this time.
>
> If your situation changes, or you see a remote role with us in future, we'd be glad to hear from you.

See our guide to [knockout questions](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/knockout-questions-how-to-screen-out-unqualified-applicants-without-losing-good-ones) for how to use them without losing good candidates.

## How to give feedback safely

Many companies avoid feedback for legal reasons. If you do share it:

- **Keep it job-related** and tied to the role's requirements.
- **Be specific and brief**: one or two points.
- **Pair a gap with a strength.**
- **Base it on your rubric**, not general impressions. Our guide to [structured interviews and scorecards](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/structured-interviews-for-tech-hiring-how-to-build-scorecards-and-rubrics) helps here.
- **Don't debate.** If a candidate disagrees, thank them and leave it there.

## Candidate experience tips beyond the rejection

- **Tell candidates your process and timeline** in the job post and first call.
- **Keep your pipeline moving.** Applications that sit untouched for weeks lead to ghosting by default.
- **Give candidates status updates** they can see without emailing you.
- **Respect their time**: short forms, reasonable assessments, and no surprise extra rounds.
- **Make rejections consistent**: the same template and timing for everyone at the same stage.

